Figuring whether it matters or not is not easy. Besides, any message has
to be either `plain' (normal) or `flowed' (flawed:) ). So, you can't
have a `flowed' paragraph of text and a `plain' diagram or screenshot.

Generally, there can be three points of view:

a) ``I'm using a real news client, sitting in front of a 80x25 console
or xterm. Naturallu I'm using a fixed-width font. I want my messages to
be easily readable by people in text mode. I don't care about those who
use those bloated GUI's. I just wrap the text at 72 or so characters and
people with normal newsreaders can read them with ease, and I don't care
if any of those moronic GUI's shows the message in the left half of the
screen or anything.''

b) ``I'm using a normal graphical/web-based news client with a
variable-width font. I want my messages to be easily readable by people
not depending on their preferred screen size. I don't care (or know)
about those who prefer console-mode newsreaders. I just write the text
in one big line and say/imply that it's text/plain, never mind it isn't
--- normal people will see my messages formatted to fit them, and I
don't care for any of those masochists.''

c) ``I'm using the ... news client, but I want everyone to see my
messages in the way that suits them best. I send messages in a format
that looks quite like text/plain with line wraps at first sight, so that
text-mode newsreaders show it correctly; but I also make the format
suitable for `un-wrapping' by graphical clients. This is not easy in
general, and the format's string can't easily begin with spaces, so I
have to add an extra space there (and in some other cases), but that's
easily reversible. So older newsreaders only display an extra space
sometimes (and if this really matters the extra spaces can be stripped
manually by using sed or the like), newer text-mode newsreaders
understand the format and show it intact, and newer graphical
newsreaders understand the format and present it to their user the way
they think best.''

Your choice?

